            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\n> could it auto-dissapear in case of a word typed in whole and without any possibilities of continuation?\n\nThe interaction with completion popup menu is done entirely using Neovim's built-in capabilities (see `:h ins-completion-menu`). After initial showing, manual typing narrows down suggested candidates. If there are no more candidates that match the typed prefix (a.k.a. base), there is no candidates shown (technical detail: but the previous completion candidates are still \"active\" if they only *just* disappeared; this is done for a more responsive `<BS>`).\n\nHere is the demo of how it works:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3310f576-5488-44a9-be7e-1d40cf27ac62\n\nIf you have a different behavior regarding how popup is shown, this is probably the result of some other settings. If you can make a screencast of how it works, we can try to find the reason.\n\n> ... but if the word is completed _by hand_ the window does not disappear and `<Enter>` has to be pressed (e.g. if there a new line should be inserted)\n\nIf the problem is with `<CR>` (i.e. `<Enter>`) in particular, then its handling is [deliberately not a part of 'mini.completion'](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/577d2dcb558efce1518c45a76345e01bf346c8e8/doc/mini-completion.txt#L58-L60) because it is such a ubiquitous key that it is usually better to be left for the user to explicitly define. The link suggests several ways of resolving this, the 'mini.keymap' approach being the one I'd go with. Here is [how it is done in MiniMax](https://github.com/nvim-mini/MiniMax/blob/70ade6783c9d45d5c81eab5a2d3ff3a62e02066e/configs/nvim-0.11/plugin/30_mini.lua#L551-L553).\n\n---\n\nClosing as it seems to work as expected. If you have any further question, feel free to post them and we'll try to figure something out.",
